A local binding gives a value a name. If two rows both contain `"Draft"`,
reading their titles gives you the same text. A helper that needs to pass
on *which title* the caller supplied asks for a `Wire of String`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    read_title |title Wire of String| -> String {
        return title
    }

    row_title |title Wire of String| -> String {
        return read_title(title)
    }

    title ~= "Draft"
    io.line(row_title(title))
    ~title = "Overthrow government"
    io.line(row_title(title))
]

The first call prints `Draft`. The assignment changes the text in the same
binding, so the second call prints `Overthrow government`. Both calls pass the name
`title` without any extra marker. The receiving parameter determines what each
call does. Inside `row_title`, passing `title` to `read_title` forwards the
original binding. Inside `read_title`, returning a `String` reads its
current text.

If `row_title` took an ordinary `String`, it could print the text, but it
couldn't pass the caller's binding to `read_title`. A Wire gives it that
ability and grants read access. The caller can still reassign its mutable
local.
